    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always found math interesting but not deeply connected to my daily life. Growing up, I was more drawn to physics and art, where math plays a role without being the focus. My parents’ academic backgrounds—especially my dad’s PhD—gave me exposure, but I never felt a strong pull toward math as a subject. I see it as a tool, not a passion.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Not usually. I’ve never felt overwhelmed by math problems, even complex ones. My anxiety tends to come from uncertainty or lack of context, not the math itself. I’ve found that running or yoga helps me reset when I feel stuck. Math doesn’t trigger stress like other subjects—maybe because I’ve used it in physics and art projects, where it feels practical and creative.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used a simple AI app to understand basic algebra concepts last year. It walked me through problems step by step, which helped me grasp patterns. It wasn’t perfect—sometimes it gave overly simplified explanations—but it made learning less intimidating. I especially liked how it adapted to my mistakes. It’s not a replacement, but a helpful companion.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b² - 4ac)] / (2a). Calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac) to see if there are real solutions. If positive, two real roots; if zero, one root; if negative, complex roots. Plug in the numbers and simplify step by step. Always check your answers by plugging them back in.",
